text:
This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:

   A. Original Stage N7 goal

   The user requested a team-owned AP NuttX image running on physical CPU1, started by CPU0/CP NuttX:

   - CPU0/CP boots physical CPU1.
   - CPU1 runs an independent single-core NuttX image with `CONFIG_SMP=n`.
   - Physical CPU1 is AP logical core 0.
   - AP vector anchor is `__vector_core0_table`.
   - Required artifacts:
     - CP raw image: `app.bin`
     - AP raw image: `app1.bin`
   - Preserve the LittleFS partition.
   - CPU0 release sequence equivalent to:

     ```c
     sys_drv_set_cpu1_pwr_dw(0);
     sys_drv_set_cpu1_rxevt_sel(1);
     sys_drv_set_cpu1_boot_address_offset(ap_base >> 8);
     sys_drv_set_cpu1_reset(1);
     ```

   - AP reports READY through shared SRAM plus a mailbox doorbell.
   - Board validation was deferred.
   - CPU2, AP SMP, RPTUN/OpenAMP/RPMsg, Wi-Fi, BLE, flashing, and unrelated refactors were explicitly excluded.

   The N7 direct startup prototype was completed, built, reviewed, documented, and committed as:

   ```text
   38699e8 feat(bk7258): add CPU1 AP bring-up prototype
   ```

   It was not pushed.

   B. Architectural correction

   The user pointed out that the agreed AP direction was wrapper-based and that the N7 prototype did not compile/link AP SDK libraries and still used direct MMIO.

   Accepted final architecture:

   ```text
   NuttX
     -> team-owned AP wrapper
       -> AP SDK/HAL libraries
         -> BK7258 hardware
   ```

   The current N7 state remains:

   ```text
   Direct CPU1 startup prototype: build-verified, pending board test
   Final AP wrapper architecture: not yet implemented
   ```

   The user decided:

   - Keep and commit the N7 prototype.
   - They will perform board validation later.
   - After validation, begin wrapper implementation.
   - During wrapper implementation:
     - do not use skills;
     - do not use subagents;
     - the main model works directly.

   C. UART wrapper work

   The user then rewrote the CPU0 UART lower-half following:

   ```text
   contest2026_135_yongwangzhiqian/docs/SDK_WRAPPER_UART_教程.md
   ```

   They rebuilt `libdriver.a` so `uart_driver.c.obj` was compiled with:

   ```text
   -DCONFIG_BK_PRINTF_DISABLE
   ```

   This removes the call to `bk_printf_init()` from `bk_uart_driver_init()`.

   Runtime problems reported and resolved:

   1. NuttX entered but stopped printing.
   2. After fixing initialization, pressing Enter produced:

      ```text
      nsh> [uart]
      kfifo data is empty
      ```

   The user confirmed the second problem was also fixed and then explicitly requested:

   > “已经修复开始评审 /home/lijian/project/open-vela/.worktrees/bk7258-clean-pr/board/bk7258_t5ai/chip/bk7258_serial.c 必要的地方可以补充，然后提交代码”

   Current required outcome:

   - Review the clean-worktree UART wrapper.
   - Apply only necessary fixes/supplements.
   - Verify it.
   - Commit the code.
   - Do not push unless separately requested.

2. Key Technical Concepts:

   - BK7258/T5-AI multi-core roles:
     - physical CPU0 = CP;
     - physical CPU1 = AP logical core 0.
   - Independent CP and AP NuttX images.
   - NuttX UART upper-half/lower-half interface:
     - `struct uart_dev_s`
     - `struct uart_ops_s`
     - `.setup`
     - `.attach`
     - `.receive`
     - `.rxavailable`
     - `.send`
     - `.txint`
   - NuttX console initialization semantics:
     - `uart_open()` deliberately skips `uart_setup()` when `dev->isconsole == true`.
     - Console hardware must already be initialized before first open.
   - Beken SDK UART APIs:
     - `bk_uart_driver_init()`
     - `bk_uart_init()`
     - `bk_uart_disable_sw_fifo()`
     - `bk_uart_set_rx_full_threshold()`
     - `bk_uart_register_rx_isr()`
     - `bk_uart_enable_rx_interrupt()`
     - `bk_uart_disable_rx_interrupt()`
     - `bk_uart_read_bytes()`
     - `bk_uart_write_bytes()`
     - `bk_uart_is_tx_over()`
   - SDK-to-NuttX IRQ bridge:
     - `CONFIG_BK7258_SDK_IRQ_BRIDGE=y`
     - `bk_int_isr_register()` maps SDK interrupt sources to NuttX IRQ handlers.
   - SDK software RX FIFO versus NuttX RX ring:
     - `bk_uart_init()` enables the SDK software FIFO by default.
     - Disabling it leaves bytes in the hardware FIFO for `uart_recvchars()`.
   - One-byte RX lookahead:
     - NuttX separates `rxavailable()` from `receive()`.
     - Beken’s read API consumes the byte.
     - A `rxbyte` cache bridges the semantic mismatch.
   - Static archive behavior:
     - `libdriver.a(uart_driver.c.obj)` contains all UART driver functions.
     - Replacing that member preserves the archive ABI while changing compile-time behavior.
   - `CONFIG_BK_PRINTF_DISABLE`:
     - It is added when compiling `uart_driver.c`.
     - It compiles out only the `bk_printf_init()` call.
     - It does not remove the UART APIs.
   - Proprietary SDK bundle policy:
     - `board/bk7258_t5ai/bk_idk/armino_as_lib/` is gitignored.
     - The modified `libdriver.a` is local and must not be committed.
     - Its checksum intentionally differs from the tracked SDK manifest.
   - NuttX style gates:
     - `git diff --check`
     - `tools/checkpatch.sh -f`
     - Overlay file-header path errors are a known false positive.
   - Wrapper-phase execution constraint:
     - no skill;
     - no subagent;
     - no Workflow;
     - main model performs the work directly.

3. Files and Code Sections:

   A. Active reviewed file

   `/home/lijian/project/open-vela/.worktrees/bk7258-clean-pr/board/bk7258_t5ai/chip/bk7258_serial.c`

   This is the file the user explicitly requested to review and commit.

   The original worktree version had already incorporated the two board fixes but still contained stale direct-MMIO comments, style problems, unused parameters, trailing whitespace, inconsistent indentation, and weak error-expression formatting.

   Key final reviewed design:

   ```c
   struct bk7258_uart_s
   {
     uart_id_t id;
     int rxbyte;
   };
   ```

   The one-byte `rxbyte` cache is retained because `bk_uart_read_bytes()` consumes data while NuttX calls `rxavailable()` before `receive()`.

   Current reviewed setup logic:

   ```c
   static int bk7258_uart_setup(struct uart_dev_s *dev)
   {
     static bool initialized;
     struct bk7258_uart_s *priv = dev->priv;
     const uart_config_t config =
       {
         .baud_rate = BK7258_UART_BAUD_RATE,
         .data_bits = UART_DATA_8_BITS,
         .parity = UART_PARITY_NONE,
         .stop_bits = UART_STOP_BITS_1,
         .flow_ctrl = UART_FLOWCTRL_DISABLE,
         .src_clk = UART_SCLK_XTAL_26M,
         .rx_dma_en = UART_DMA_DISABLE,
         .tx_dma_en = UART_DMA_DISABLE,
       };

     if (initialized)
       {
         return OK;
       }

     if (bk_uart_driver_init() != BK_OK)
       {
         return -EIO;
       }

     if (bk_uart_init(priv->id, &config) != BK_OK)
       {
         return -EIO;
       }

     /* NuttX owns the receive ring.  Keep bytes in the hardware FIFO so the SDK
      * callback can hand them directly to uart_recvchars().
      */

     if (bk_uart_disable_sw_fifo(priv->id) != BK_OK)
       {
         return -EIO;
       }

     if (bk_uart_set_rx_full_threshold(priv->id, 1) != BK_OK)
       {
         return -EIO;
       }

     priv->rxbyte = -1;
     initialized = true;
     return OK;
   }
   ```

   Important properties:

   - `initialized` is set only after every initialization step succeeds.
   - SDK software FIFO is disabled after `bk_uart_init()`.
   - RX threshold is set to one byte.
   - No SDK initialization occurs in `arm_earlyserialinit()`.

   SDK ISR callback:

   ```c
   static void bk7258_uart_sdk_isr(uart_id_t id, void *param)
   {
     struct uart_dev_s *dev = (struct uart_dev_s *)param;

     (void)id;
     uart_recvchars(dev);
   }
   ```

   Attach/detach path:

   ```c
   static int bk7258_uart_attach(struct uart_dev_s *dev)
   {
     struct bk7258_uart_s *priv = dev->priv;
     bk_err_t ret;

     ret = bk_uart_register_rx_isr(priv->id, bk7258_uart_sdk_isr, dev);
     return ret == BK_OK ? OK : -EIO;
   }

   static void bk7258_uart_detach(struct uart_dev_s *dev)
   {
     struct bk7258_uart_s *priv = dev->priv;

     (void)bk_uart_disable_rx_interrupt(priv->id);
     (void)bk_uart_register_rx_isr(priv->id, NULL, NULL);
   }
   ```

   RX path:

   ```c
   static bool bk7258_uart_rxavailable(struct uart_dev_s *dev)
   {
     struct bk7258_uart_s *priv = dev->priv;

     if (priv->rxbyte < 0)
       {
         uint8_t byte;
         int nread;

         nread = bk_uart_read_bytes(priv->id, &byte, 1, 0);
         priv->rxbyte = nread == 1 ? byte : -1;
       }

     return priv->rxbyte >= 0;
   }

   static int bk7258_uart_receive(struct uart_dev_s *dev,
                                  unsigned int *status)
   {
     struct bk7258_uart_s *priv = dev->priv;
     int ch = priv->rxbyte;

     priv->rxbyte = -1;
     if (status)
       {
         *status = 0;
       }

     return ch;
   }
   ```

   TX path:

   ```c
   static void bk7258_uart_send(struct uart_dev_s *dev, int ch)
   {
     struct bk7258_uart_s *priv = dev->priv;
     uint8_t byte = (uint8_t)ch;

     (void)bk_uart_write_bytes(priv->id, &byte, 1);
   }

   static void bk7258_uart_txint(struct uart_dev_s *dev, bool enable)
   {
     if (enable)
       {
         uart_xmitchars(dev);
       }
   }

   static bool bk7258_uart_txready(struct uart_dev_s *dev)
   {
     /* bk_uart_write_bytes() waits for hardware FIFO space. */

     (void)dev;
     return true;
   }

   static bool bk7258_uart_txempty(struct uart_dev_s *dev)
   {
     struct bk7258_uart_s *priv = dev->priv;

     return bk_uart_is_tx_over(priv->id);
   }
   ```

   Corrected console initialization:

   ```c
   #ifdef USE_EARLYSERIALINIT
   void arm_earlyserialinit(void)
   {
     /* Early output uses bk7258_lowputc.c.  SDK setup is deferred until
      * arm_serialinit(), when allocation and semaphore services are available.
      */

     CONSOLE_DEV.isconsole = true;
   }
   #endif

   #ifdef USE_SERIALDRIVER
   void arm_serialinit(void)
   {
     CONSOLE_DEV.isconsole = true;

     if (bk7258_uart_setup(&CONSOLE_DEV) < 0)
       {
         return;
       }

     (void)uart_register("/dev/console", &CONSOLE_DEV);
   }
   #endif
   ```

   This is critical because NuttX skips `.setup()` when opening an object marked as the console.

   Other cleanup applied:

   - Replaced stale direct-register architecture description with SDK-wrapper architecture.
   - Removed unused:
     - `<nuttx/irq.h>`
     - `<arch/irq.h>`
   - Preserved `"arm_internal.h"` for ARM serial build macros.
   - Converted mixed Chinese implementation comments to concise surrounding-style English.
   - Added explicit `(void)` casts for intentionally unused parameters and ignored return values.
   - Removed trailing whitespace.
   - Wrapped long prototypes.
   - Added missing blank lines and final newline.

   B. NuttX serial upper half

   `nuttx/drivers/serial/serial.c`

   Critical behavior:

   ```c
   if (tmp == 1)
     {
       irqstate_t flags = uart_spinlock(dev, false);

       /* If this is the console, then the UART has already been initialized. */

       if (!dev->isconsole)
         {
           ret = uart_setup(dev);
           ...
         }

       ret = uart_attach(dev);
       ...
       uart_enablerxint(dev);
     }
   ```

   This proved the tutorial’s original statement “setup runs on first `/dev/console` open” was wrong.

   `nuttx/drivers/serial/serial_io.c`

   `uart_recvchars()` loops:

   ```c
